sections.wikitext:
[[File:Get-Around.png|thumb|upright=1.2|Transcription of the opening vocals in "I Get Around"]]

"I Get Around" begins in the key of [[G major]] with an ''a cappella'' intro section that contains a faster version of the chord progression used in the chorus: I–VI–ii–{{music|flat}}VII–V.{{sfn|Lambert|2007|p=149}} Love stated that he had based the intro on [[The Regents (doo-wop band)|the Regents]]' 1961 hit "[[Barbara Ann|Barbara-Ann]]", a song that the Beach Boys later covered on their 1965 album ''[[Beach Boys' Party!]]''.{{sfn|Love|2016|p=160}}

The verses alternate between ii and V, one of Wilson's favorite progressions, which he had previously used in "[[Little Deuce Coupe (song)|Little Deuce Coupe]]" and "[[Little Saint Nick]]".{{sfn|Lambert|2016|p=74}}{{sfn|Lambert|2007|p=149}} Noting the common theme between those songs and "I Get Around", musicologist [[Philip Lambert]] remarks, "Brian [had] quite literally updated the [[car song]]."{{sfn|Lambert|2007|p=149}} Wilson cited [[the Chiffons]]' "[[One Fine Day (song)|One Fine Day]]" (1963) as another possible musical influence on "I Get Around".{{sfn|Lambert|2007|p=149}}

During the instrumental solo, the key modulates to [[A major]], which is then followed by a descent to [[A-flat major|A{{music|flat}} major]].{{sfn|Lambert|2007|p=149}}  Biographer [[Peter Ames Carlin]] distinguished the song for its "jarring rhythmic shifts, fuzz guitar, off-kilter organ riffs, and Brian's own wailing [[falsetto]]",{{sfn|Carlin|2006|p=43}} while [[David Leaf]] called it "a major, revolutionary step in Brian's use of dynamics".<ref name="Leaf1990">{{cite AV media notes| title = Little Deuce Coupe / All Summer Long | others= [[The Beach Boys]]| year = 1990| first = David| last = Leaf|author-link=David Leaf|publisher=[[Capitol Records]]|type=CD Liner|url=http://albumlinernotes.com/Little_Deuce_Coupe.html}}</ref>

