sections.wikitext:
[[File:Sullivan Beach Boys.jpg|thumb|right|upright=1.3|alt=The Beach Boys performing, flanked by muscle cars|The Beach Boys performing "I Get Around" on ''[[The Ed Sullivan Show]]'' (September 27, 1964){{sfn|Badman|2004|p=66}}]]

"I Get Around", backed with "[[Don't Worry Baby]]", was released as a single in the U.S. on May 11, 1964.{{sfn|Badman|2004|p=55}} ''[[Cash Box]]'' described it as an "exciting, tailored-for-teen- tastes hot rod stomp'er...that should be getting around at a quick clip."<ref name=cb>{{cite magazine |title=CashBox Record Reviews |date=May 16, 1964 |page=10 |access-date=2022-01-12 |url=https://worldradiohstory.com/Archive-All-Music/Cash-Box/60s/1964/CB-1964-05-16.pdf |magazine=Cash Box}}{{dead link|date=May 2025|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>

On June 6, "I Get Around" debuted on the ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'' Top 40 chart at number 17.{{sfn|Badman|2004|p=57}} Four weeks later, on July 4, it overtook [[Peter and Gordon]]'s "[[A World Without Love]]" to become the Beach Boys' first chart-topping U.S. hit.{{sfn|Badman|2004|p=59}} Coincidentally, this had occurred on the same day as America's annual [[Independence Day (United States)|Independence Day]] holiday.{{sfn|Dillon|2012|p=38}} The single maintained the number-one position for two weeks.{{sfn|Love|2016|p=161}}

According to author James Perone, "I Get Around" represented both a successful response by Wilson to the [[British Invasion]], and the beginning of an unofficial rivalry between him and the Beatles, principally [[Paul McCartney]].{{sfn|Perone|2015|pp=42, 47}} Lambert concurred, "As 'I Get Around' rose to the top of the charts ... the Beatles surely recognized that they had a formidable rival."{{sfn|Lambert|2007|p=150}} In the UK, the single was released in June 1964 and reached number 7 on the ''[[Record Retailer]]'' chart{{sfn|Badman|2004|pp=55, 57}} – the band's first UK hit to breach the top ten.{{sfn|Dillon|2012|p=38}} [[Rolling Stones]] frontman [[Mick Jagger]] gave the song a positive endorsement during an appearance on the British television program ''[[Ready Steady Go!]]''.{{sfn|Badman|2004|p=57}}

On July 13, 1964, ''All Summer Long'' was released with "I Get Around" as the opening track.{{sfn|Badman|2004|pp=59, 62}} A recording of the group's August 1 performance of "I Get Around" at the Civil Memorial Auditorium in [[Sacramento, California]] was included on the live album ''[[Beach Boys Concert]]'', released in October.{{sfn|Badman|2004|pp=62, 67}}

